SECTION 1: VARIABLES IN JAVASCRIPT
--------------------------------------------------------------------
A variable is a container used to store data.

JavaScript provides three ways to create variables:
• let
• const
• var

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Using let
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
let name = "Rohit";
let age = 20;

Printing:
console.log(name, age);

The let keyword allows reassignment. The value stored inside the variable
can be changed later.

Example:
age = 30;

Now age becomes 30.

JavaScript philosophy here is simple: do not overcomplicate things.
Let the brain do simple math and reassignment.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Using const
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
const account = 1234;

If we try:
account = 23;

An error occurs.

The reason is simple. const means constant. Once a value is assigned,
it cannot be changed.

Important rule:
A const variable must be assigned a value at the time of declaration.

Example:
const x;   // Error

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Using var (OLD WAY)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
var a = 10;
var a = 20;   // Allowed (bad design)

Problems with var:

1. Redeclaration is allowed
The same variable name can be declared multiple times.

2. Scope issues
var does not respect block scope.

Example:
if (true) {
    var x = 20;
}
console.log(x);   // Works (bad behavior)

var only respects:
• Global scope
• Function scope

It ignores block scope {}, which leads to unexpected bugs and memory
confusion.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Why let and const are Preferred
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• No redeclaration
• Block scope respected
• Predictable behavior

Example:
if (true) {
    let c = 90;
}
console.log(c);   // Error (correct behavior)

