SECTION 2: DATA TYPES IN JAVASCRIPT
--------------------------------------------------------------------
JavaScript data types are divided into:
1. Primitive Data Types
2. Non-Primitive Data Types

--------------------------------------------------------------------
PRIMITIVE DATA TYPES (7)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Number
2. String
3. Boolean
4. Undefined
5. Null
6. BigInt
7. Symbol

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Number
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
let a = 10;
let b = 2.36;

JavaScript does not require explicit type declaration. It automatically
decides the type based on the value.

All numbers, whether integers or decimals, belong to the number type.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
String
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
let msg = "Strike is coming";
let name = 'Anjali';

Strings can be written using single quotes or double quotes. Both are
valid in JavaScript.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Boolean
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Boolean values can only be:
true or false

Example:
let isLoggedIn = true;
let isAdmin = false;

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Undefined
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Undefined means a variable is declared but no value is assigned.

Example:
let user;
console.log(user);   // undefined

Undefined is assigned by JavaScript automatically.

A const variable cannot be undefined because it must have a value at
declaration time.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Null
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Null represents an intentional absence of value.

Example:
let weather = null;

Difference:
undefined → system says value does not exist
null → developer intentionally assigns nothing

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Real-World Analogy (Important)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
City temperature example:

If city exists and temperature is found → return 25
If city exists but temperature is not available → return null
If city does not exist → return undefined

--------------------------------------------------------------------
BigInt
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Normal numbers use 64 bits of memory.

Maximum safe integer is:
2^53 - 1

For extremely large numbers, BigInt is used.

Example:
let bigNum = 12345678901234567890n;

The n at the end makes it a BigInt.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Symbol
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Symbols are used to create unique values.

Example:
const id1 = Symbol('id');
const id2 = Symbol('id');
console.log(id2==id1);

Even though descriptions are same, symbols are always unique.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: typeof OPERATOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The typeof operator is used to check data type.

Examples:
typeof 10        → number
typeof "hi"      → string
typeof true      → boolean
typeof undefined → undefined
typeof symbol    → symbol
typeof null      → object (sabse bada bug hai known as 'legecy bug')

typeof null returning object is a known JavaScript bug kept for
backward compatibility.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
NON-PRIMITIVE DATA TYPES
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• Array
• Object
• Function

All non-primitive types return typeof object (functions are special).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Array
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
let arr = [10, 20, 30, "Rohit", true];
console.log(arr);

Arrays can store multiple values and mixed data types.

Arrays are mutable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Object- are the foundation
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Objects store data in key-value pairs.

Example:
let user = {
    name: "Rohit",
    age: 18,
    account: 12312,
    category: "General"
};

Objects give structure and meaning to data.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Function as Data Type
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Functions are first-class citizens in JavaScript.

example :
let s = function add()
        {
        console.log("Hello);
        }
console.log(s);

They can be stored in variables, passed as arguments, and returned from
other functions.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: IMMUTABILITY VS MUTABILITY
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Primitive data types are immutable.

Example: let a = 10;
         a = 20;

The value 10 is not changed. A new value 20 is created at a new memory
location.

Strings are also immutable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Non-Primitive Data Types (in sab ka type of array hi niklega)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Arrays and objects are mutable.

Example:
let arr = [10, 20, 30];
arr[0] = 70;

Mutation is allowed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: COPY BY VALUE VS COPY BY REFERENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Primitive types follow copy by value.

Example:
let a = 10;
let b = a;
b = 20;

a and b are stored at different memory locations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Non-Primitive Types Follow Copy by Reference
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
let obj1 = { name: "Mohan", age: 20 };
let obj2 = obj1;

obj2.name = "Rohan";

Both obj1 and obj2 point to the same memory location.

This avoids unnecessary memory duplication.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
FINAL TAKEAWAYS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• JavaScript prioritizes simplicity
• Primitive values are immutable
• Non-primitive values are mutable
• var is outdated
• let and const are modern standards
• Understanding memory behavior is a senior-level skill

